<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batangas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Batangas is a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, with its capital at Batangas City and Taal Volcano within its borders. Its people took a prominent part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and in resistance to American rule during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>. In 1901–1902 US forces under General J. Franklin Bell used harsh reconcentration measures there to cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> off from support.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batangas.jpg" alt="Batangas" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: patrickroque01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batangas_Capitol_(side_view)_(J.P._Laurel_Highway,_Batangas_City)(2018-07-30).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Batangas lies in the south-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, in the Calabarzon region, bordered by Cavite and Laguna to the north and Quezon to the east. Across the Verde Island Passage to the south is the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindoro">Mindoro</a>, and to the west lies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. Its landscape is largely plains dotted by mountains, among them Mount Taal, one of the world's smallest volcanoes, which stands in the middle of Taal Lake. The name comes from batangan, which means either a log found in the Calumpang River or the rafts used to fish in Taal Lake. The province is also known poetically by its ancient name, Kumintáng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Large centres of population already stood along the coasts and rivers, with barangays lining the Pansipit River, which drains Bombon Lake, now Taal. The area was a major site of the Maritime Jade Road, a sea-based trade network that ran for about 3,000 years from about 2000 BCE to about 1000 CE. Kumintang was a large polity around the Calumpang River; local tradition names Gat Pulintan as its paramount datu, who refused to convert and resisted the Spanish from the hills. In 1570 the Spanish generals Martin de Goiti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salcedo">Juan de Salcedo</a> explored the coast on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, and the town of Taal was founded in 1572.</p><p>Balayan was the provincial capital from 1597 to 1732, when it moved to Taal. The Great Taal Eruption of 1754 destroyed that capital, and in 1889 the seat moved to Batangas City. Under American rule the province was one of the places where revolutionaries staged plays; after a performance of Tanikalang Ginto in the province the company and the whole audience were arrested, and the play was banned throughout the country. On 12 December 1941 Jesús Villamor led six Filipino fighters against 54 Japanese bombers and escorts raiding the Batangas airfield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The province has 29 municipalities and 5 cities, with its capital at Batangas City, and the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 2,994,795 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalogs</a> are the predominant people, and the Batangas dialect of Tagalog resembles the Old Tagalog spoken before the Spanish arrived. Musicologists identify Batangas as the origin of the kumintang, an ancient war song that later evolved into the kundiman, the signature of Filipino love songs. The town of Taal keeps ancestral houses, churches and other buildings from the 19th century, and the province is known for the balisong, or butterfly knife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Batangas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic had a constitution, a cabinet and a flag, and it organised guerrilla bands that raided towns in Rizal, Cavite, Laguna and <b>Batangas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-katagalugan">Republic of Katagalugan</a></li></ul></section>
